## The idea in thirty seconds

You've probably heard that **moving clocks run slow**, and that **clocks near heavy things like planets and stars run slow too**. Physicists have measured both with atomic clocks on airplanes and satellites. GPS has to correct for them or it would drift by miles every day.

The standard story treats these as strange facts about *time itself*. This theory asks a simpler question:

> What if time isn't stretching at all? What if the "slow clock" effect is just what **distance** looks like when you measure it along a **slope**?

Think of a hiking trail on a mountainside. On a map, the trail covers one mile. But you walked farther than a mile, because part of your walk was uphill. The steeper the hill, the bigger the gap between the map distance and the real distance you covered.

The theory says that **space has an extra direction we don't normally notice**, and that moving fast, or sitting near something heavy, means you're on a *tilted* path through it. The tilt is the whole story. A steeper tilt means a slower clock.

## A trail map for the idea

<Card title="The three-line version" desc="If you remember nothing else">

1. **Speed is a tilt.** The faster you go, the steeper your path through the extra direction, and the slower your clock ticks.
2. **Gravity is a tilt.** Space near a heavy object is tilted like the inside of a funnel. Closer to the center means steeper, which means slower clocks.
3. **The expanding Universe is a tilt.** The same tilt, applied to the whole cosmos, describes why distant galaxies fly away from us.

Three famous effects. One idea.
</Card>

## Speed, as a slope

In 1905 Einstein showed that a clock moving at speed $v$ ticks slower by a factor called gamma:

$$ {#gamma}
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}}
$$

That formula looks like it came from nowhere. The paper behind this note shows that it's actually the **hypotenuse of a right triangle**:

$$ {#lorentz-slope}
\gamma = \sqrt{1 + (\text{slope})^2}
$$

Faster means steeper. As you approach the speed of light, the slope heads toward vertical, and your clock slows to a crawl. Nothing mysterious has to happen to time. You're just walking up an increasingly steep hill.

<Image
    src="https://flusterapp.com/assets/diagrams/showcase/slope_fan.png"
    alt="Left: a fan of right triangles with a common base, steeper for larger velocity. Right: gamma, beta-gamma and 1/gamma plotted against velocity as a fraction of the speed of light."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Faster means steeper. As speed approaches the speed of light, the slope shoots upward without limit."
/>

## Gravity, as a funnel

Now picture the space around a star or a planet. In this theory, that space isn't just "curved" in some abstract way. It's a real surface with a real tilt, shaped like a funnel. This shape has been known since 1916, and it's called the **Flamm paraboloid**.

Far from the mass, the funnel is nearly flat, and clocks tick normally. Closer in, the walls get steeper, and clocks slow down. At the very edge of a black hole, the wall goes straight up, and the clock stops.

<Image
    src="https://flusterapp.com/assets/diagrams/showcase/tilt_profile.png"
    alt="Left: the funnel profile with tangent segments colored by tilt, steepest near the center. Right: three curves showing how the tilt changes the clock rate with distance."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Left: how steeply space is tilted at each distance from the center. Right: what that tilt does to clocks."
/>

The relationship is the same trigonometry you may remember from school:

$$ {#tilt}
\sin\theta = \sqrt{\frac{r_s}{R}}, \qquad \cos\theta = \sqrt{1 - \frac{r_s}{R}}
$$

The cosine of the tilt angle **is** the clock rate. Gravitational time dilation drops out of a protractor.

## Falling in and flying apart are the same thing

This is the part that tends to stop people mid-sentence.

When something falls toward a planet, it speeds up. When distant galaxies drift away from us, they speed up the farther away they are. These look like opposites: one is *toward*, the other is *away*.

In this picture they are **the exact same equation**. Plug in the mass of a planet and you get the speed of something falling toward it. Plug in the mass of a ball of ordinary cosmic material and you get the expansion of the Universe. The Universe flying apart and an apple falling from a tree are two readings of one relation.

<Image
    src="https://flusterapp.com/assets/diagrams/showcase/two_flows_dark.png"
    alt="Log-log plot of flow speed against distance. Infall decreases with distance, Hubble flow increases, and they cross at a particular radius."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Infall (pink) and cosmic expansion (blue) for a galaxy. They're drawn from the same relation, and there's even a distance where they match."
/>

## One shape, three scales

The same idea draws a funnel around a point mass, a rounded dome inside a star, and a single curve for the entire Universe. One rule, wildly different sizes.

<Image
    src="https://flusterapp.com/assets/diagrams/ai_generated/three_geometries.png"
    alt="Left: a uniform-density star, with a spherical cap inside and a paraboloid outside. Right: the whole universe at critical density."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="A star and a Universe from one relation."
/>

## "Okay, but does it actually work?"

Fair question, and the right one to ask. Here is the honest answer.

Where the theory can be checked, it **reuses the equations of general relativity** and gets the same answers as the textbooks. That's a consistency check, not a new discovery, and the paper says so directly. For example, it predicts that Mercury's orbit slowly rotates by about **43 arcseconds per century**, which is exactly what astronomers observe.

<Image
    src="https://flusterapp.com/assets/diagrams/showcase/orbit_rosette.png"
    alt="A precessing orbit drawn as overlapping purple ellipses around a pink central mass."
    maxHeight="large"
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="An exaggerated orbit, where the closest point creeps around a little more each lap. Mercury does the same thing, just far more gently."
/>

<Card title="Where things stand" desc="What's solid, what's assumed, and what's a guess" sidebar right>

**Solid:** if you accept general relativity's equations, the slope picture reproduces gravitational time dilation, black-hole geometry, and Mercury's orbit. The algebra has also been independently re-checked in the Lean 4 proof assistant.<br />

**Assumed:** that there is a preferred "absolute" frame tied to the expansion of the Universe.<br />

**A guess:** a proposal about why galaxies spin faster than their visible matter should allow. It could be wrong, and the paper says so.
</Card>

This is an independent researcher's preprint. It has **not** been through peer review, and the author is the first to say that the interesting claims need to be tested. That's the point of sharing it.

## Try it yourself

Reading about slopes is one thing. Dragging one around is another. The **Lorentz Slope** companion app turns each idea above into an interactive screen with live sliders. If you're on a Mac, you can orbit the funnel, move a probe in and out, and watch the clock rate change as you go.

